{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "llm-inference-gateway",
  "title": "LLM Inference Gateway: Routing & Fallback",
  "description": "LLM inference gateway: how one client endpoint turns a capability-tier request into a routed, cached, streamed and metered response, and how it behaves under overload and provider failure.\n\n**Layout.** A two-row ring. Top row, left to right, is the request path: Client Apps → Edge & Admission (edge service, admission controller, deadline-aware queue, explicit reject) → Prompt & Cache (prompt assembler, response cache) → Routing & Protection (tier router, shadow mirror, circuit-breaker states Closed → Open → Half-open). Below, right to left, the response comes back: Fallback (under Routing: tokens-already-sent decision → failure classifier → retryable decision) → Backend Capacity (hosted provider accounts A/B, self-hosted pool with scheduler and replicas, shadow backend) → Response Streaming (token streamer plus the caller-facing failure ends: visible stream failure, return error now) → Accounting (spend guard, metering, billing stream, audit store, one column under the client).\n\n**Key flows.** Cache miss → router → backends; backend tokens → streamer → client; backend failure → \"tokens already sent?\" (yes: fail visibly, keep sent tokens; no: classify → retryable? yes: back to the router with the next fallback, a fresh deadline and the same idempotency key; no: return immediately). The classifier feeds the breaker errors and timeouts; an open breaker drains traffic at the router. At the spend ceiling the spend guard first has the router use the cheapest backend, then has admission reject new work.\n\n**Color key.** blue = request/response step, violet = control decision, teal = model backend, green = store or stream, orange = shadow evaluation, red = reject or failure.",
  "mode": "diagram",
